{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011sfyRgNSWdybzAewjeUd5r","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CoRL openreview review form \"overall recommendation\" \"strong accept\" \"accept\" \"reject\" scale robotics\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Reviewer Guidelines — CoRL 2023\",\"url\":\"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2026 - Instruction for Authors\",\"url\":\"https://www.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-authors\"},{\"title\":\"Default Review Form | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://docs.openreview.net/reference/default-forms/default-review-form\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2024 - Instruction for Reviews\",\"url\":\"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews\"},{\"title\":\"OpenReviewer: A Specialized Large Language Model for Generating Critical Scientific Paper Reviews\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.11948v3\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2026 Conference | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/group?id=robot-learning.org%2FCoRL%2F2026%2FConference\"},{\"title\":\"ICLR 2022 Reviewer Instructions\",\"url\":\"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2022/ReviewerGuide\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2023 Conference | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/group?id=robot-learning.org%2FCoRL%2F2023%2FConference\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2021 - Submission Instructions\",\"url\":\"https://2021.corl.org/author-information/submission-instructions\"},{\"title\":\"Review Process · Robotics: Science and Systems\",\"url\":\"https://roboticsconference.org/reviewps/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about OpenReview's review form and CoRL's review process:\n\n## Overall Recommendation Scale\n\nOpenReview's default review form uses a rating scale with the following options: \"10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\", \"9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\", \"8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\", \"7: Good paper, accept\", \"6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\", \"5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\", \"4: Ok but not good enough - rejection\", \"3: Clear rejection\", \"2: Strong rejection\", and \"1: Trivial or wrong\".\n\n## CoRL Review Guidelines\n\nFor CoRL specifically, the main review form asks for an evaluation of the originality, quality, clarity, significance, relevance and limitations of the work, including whether the problems or methods are new and if the work is a novel combination of well-known techniques.\n\nPapers that receive all reviews in the \"reject\" range in the initial round will not proceed to the rebuttal phase, indicating that CoRL uses distinct reject/accept thresholds in their review scale.\n\nThe search results show that CoRL uses OpenReview's platform for managing submissions and reviews, though the specific numerical scale used by CoRL may have its own customizations. The standard OpenReview scale ranges from 1-10 with the descriptors you mentioned (strong accept, accept, reject, etc.) mapped to different score ranges.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"808481c6-6a8a-4eae-a76b-0f8df33193a6","uuid":"b9a9a7a8-133a-42b6-b304-ca1dfd17f482","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:36:18.630Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CoRL openreview review form \"overall recommendation\" \"strong accept\" \"accept\" \"reject\" scale robotics","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01E6VtELmWQUfAiz5XQBZjkB","content":[{"title":"Reviewer Guidelines — CoRL 2023","url":"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines"},{"title":"CoRL 2026 - Instruction for Authors","url":"https://www.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-authors"},{"title":"Default Review Form | OpenReview","url":"https://docs.openreview.net/reference/default-forms/default-review-form"},{"title":"CoRL 2024 - Instruction for Reviews","url":"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews"},{"title":"OpenReviewer: A Specialized Large Language Model for Generating Critical Scientific Paper Reviews","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.11948v3"},{"title":"CoRL 2026 Conference | OpenReview","url":"https://openreview.net/group?id=robot-learning.org%2FCoRL%2F2026%2FConference"},{"title":"ICLR 2022 Reviewer Instructions","url":"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2022/ReviewerGuide"},{"title":"CoRL 2023 Conference | OpenReview","url":"https://openreview.net/group?id=robot-learning.org%2FCoRL%2F2023%2FConference"},{"title":"CoRL 2021 - Submission Instructions","url":"https://2021.corl.org/author-information/submission-instructions"},{"title":"Review Process · Robotics: Science and Systems","url":"https://roboticsconference.org/reviewps/"}]},"Based on the search results, I found relevant information about OpenReview's review form and CoRL's review process:\n\n## Overall Recommendation Scale\n\nOpenReview's default review form uses a rating scale with the following options: \"10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\", \"9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\", \"8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\", \"7: Good paper, accept\", \"6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\", \"5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\", \"4: Ok but not good enough - rejection\", \"3: Clear rejection\", \"2: Strong rejection\", and \"1: Trivial or wrong\".\n\n## CoRL Review Guidelines\n\nFor CoRL specifically, the main review form asks for an evaluation of the originality, quality, clarity, significance, relevance and limitations of the work, including whether the problems or methods are new and if the work is a novel combination of well-known techniques.\n\nPapers that receive all reviews in the \"reject\" range in the initial round will not proceed to the rebuttal phase, indicating that CoRL uses distinct reject/accept thresholds in their review scale.\n\nThe search results show that CoRL uses OpenReview's platform for managing submissions and reviews, though the specific numerical scale used by CoRL may have its own customizations. The standard OpenReview scale ranges from 1-10 with the descriptors you mentioned (strong accept, accept, reject, etc.) mapped to different score ranges."],"durationSeconds":6.270152165}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Q2TetuLPG4JGtn2vwT5aL9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I now have the CoRL review scoring scale based on OpenReview's default form, which CoRL uses. Let me compile:\n\n**CoRL Review Scale (1-10):**\n- 10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\n- 9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough - rejection\n\n\n- 3: Clear rejection\n- 2: Strong rejection\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nThe review dimensions I'll be evaluating are Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, and Limitations. With CoRL's historical acceptance rate around 30%, I now have what I need to move forward. I'll dispatch the first reviewer to assess methods and experiments while the second reviewer examines novelty and related work in parallel, then we'll conduct the roundtable discussion. Let me start by summarizing the paper.\n\nThe paper introduces IMM, a robustness-aware compression framework for vision-language-action models that's the first to combine theoretical guarantees with practical token compression. It uses a lightweight cross-attention module to score token importance, employs two-stage training to avoid co-adaptation issues, and provides formal stability bounds on how often top-k token selections might flip under perturbations. The experimental results show it recovers most of the robustness lost through standard compression on the LIBERO-Plus benchmark, achieving 50.3% performance compared to 53.1% uncompressed while maintaining near-lossless compression.\n\nThe approach generalizes across different VLA architectures—SmolVLA and OpenVLA—with consistent improvements in robustness across multiple benchmarks including LIBERO variants and aloha_sim_insertion. The two-stage training strategy is critical, reducing co-adaptation metrics significantly compared to joint training, and the framework delivers a 1.62x inference speedup at 50% compression while being agnostic to the underlying model architecture. Validation ✓ (Table 3)\n3. plan_003: OpenVLA Model Validation ✓ (Table 4)\n4. plan_004: Training Ablation (Joint/Grad-Isolated/Two-Stage) ✓ (Table 7)\n5. plan_005: Formal Bound Validation ✓ (Table 10)\n6. plan_006: Cross-Compression Validation ✓ (Table 5)\n7. plan_007: Defense Comparison ✓ (Table 6)\n8. plan_008: Pruning Ratio Sweep ✓ (Table 8)\n9. plan_009: Token Importance Analysis ✓ (Table 11)\n10. plan_010: v8c Annealing Ablation ✓ (Table 9)\n11. plan_011: ACIS Overhead ✓ (Table 12)\n12. plan_012: LIBERO-Plus 7-Dimension ✓ (Table 1)\n\nAll experiments are accounted for across the paper. Now I'm setting up two parallel reviewers—one to evaluate the methods and experimental design, another to assess novelty and positioning relative to existing work.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1771,"cache_read_input_tokens":54785,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1771},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"808481c6-6a8a-4eae-a76b-0f8df33193a6","uuid":"257b8cf1-de7a-4ee2-a6fc-29cdc67ee567"}
