{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Q2TetuLPG4JGtn2vwT5aL9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012CQa9smLNftRTpeCXKpL9q","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Produce an independent, blind review.\n\n## CoRL Review Scale (1-10, OpenReview)\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal paper\n- 9: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough, rejection\n- 3: Clear rejection\n- 2: Strong rejection\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nReview dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations.\nCoRL acceptance rate: ~30%. The venue emphasizes robot learning with real or sim experiments, principled methods, and practical impact.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Importance Margin Maximization: A Robustness-Aware Framework for Vision-Language-Action Token Compression\n\n**Problem:** Token compression is essential for deploying VLAs on resource-constrained robots, but existing methods (FastV, VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache) are brittle—small input perturbations destabilize importance rankings and cause safety-critical tokens to be discarded.\n\n**Method — IMM (Importance Margin Maximization):**\n1. **ACIS** (Adaptive Compression Importance Scorer): A lightweight cross-attention module (2.3M params, 0.46% of backbone) that produces per-token importance scores. Given visual tokens V ∈ R^{n×d} and learned query q ∈ R^{1×d}: s_i = σ(qK_i^T/√d), K = VW_K.\n2. **Importance margin** Δ_k = s_(k) − s_(k+1) (gap between k-th and (k+1)-th largest score).\n3. **Formal bound** (Proposition 1): P(top-k flip) ≤ k·ε·L/Δ_k for L-Lipschitz scorer under perturbation ‖δ‖ ≤ ε.\n4. **Training objective:** L_margin = −log(Δ_k) + λ·L_action(â, a*)\n5. **Two-stage training:** Stage 1 freezes backbone, trains ACIS on L_margin. Stage 2 freezes ACIS, trains LoRA adapters on backbone.\n6. **Deployment:** ACIS scores tokens → top-k selection → compressed sequence to frozen backbone.\n\n**Experiments (all present in paper):**\n- Table 1: LIBERO-Plus 7-dimension perturbation (headline). IMM+Compress recovers 78% of robustness lost by standard compression (37.3%→50.3% vs 53.1% uncompressed). Avg perturbed gap: only −2.8pp vs no-compress.\n- Table 2: aloha_sim_insertion success rate (60 episodes). ACIS prune 50%: 84% vs baseline 82% (p=0.38, not significant). Random prune: 54% (p=0.001).\n- Table 3: LIBERO 3 suites. Average −0.7pp (not significant, p=0.31). G3 0.92–1.04.\n- Table 4: Cross-model (SmolVLA 0.5B vs OpenVLA 7B). SmolVLA G3=0.87, OpenVLA G3=1.09.\n- Table 5: Cross-compression plugin. FastV/VLA-IAP/VLA-Cache all gain +52–56pp preservation, G3≈1.0.\n- Table 6: Defense comparison. IMM: 67% clean / 63% @ε=0.01 / 48% @ε=0.05, 1.3x cost, with compression. AT: 62%/59%/46%, 3.2x cost, no compression.\n- Table 7: Training strategy ablation. Joint(v7) G3=3.29, Grad-Isolated(v8) G3=1.34, Two-Stage(v8b) G3=0.87.\n- Table 8: Pruning ratio sweep. U-shaped G3 curve, optimal at 0.6 (G3=0.83), sweet spot 0.5–0.7.\n- Table 9: Annealing ablation. Fixed 0.5 slightly better (G3 0.87 vs 0.93, margin 0.93 vs 0.85).\n- Table 10: Formal bound validation. Bound never violated, tightness ratio ~6.2x average.\n- Table 11: Token importance analysis. Pruned tokens have lower attention (ratio 0.44, d=0.42) and gradient (ratio 0.32, d=0.51).\n- Table 12: ACIS overhead. +0.46% params, +0.6ms latency, net −37% latency, +64.7% throughput with 50% compression.\n\n**Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml):**\nAll 12 planned experiments are covered in the paper. The paper matches the full experiment plan.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Technical soundness of the method.** Is Proposition 1 correct? Is the margin-based formulation well-motivated? Any gaps in the formal analysis?\n2. **Two-stage training.** Is the co-adaptation argument convincing? Is the ablation (Table 7) sufficient to justify the two-stage design?\n3. **Experimental rigor.** Statistical significance, sample sizes, confidence intervals, number of seeds, baselines chosen.\n4. **Completeness of experiments.** Are the key claims adequately supported? Any missing experiments?\n5. **Reproducibility.** Are enough details provided?\n6. **Weaknesses in the experimental methodology.**\n\nStructure your output as:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, be specific with table/section references]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, be specific, distinguish major vs minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n[If any]\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: X/10\n- Quality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Summary Assessment\n[1-2 sentences: accept/reject leaning and why]\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1771,"cache_read_input_tokens":54785,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1771},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"808481c6-6a8a-4eae-a76b-0f8df33193a6","uuid":"49b4eeaa-1894-4359-99a3-69c92ae07a6e"}
