{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019CKYskuEtUEyvc6vtvd5LN","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260516T075046\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 5 - marginally below acceptance threshold\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-16T08:35:04.661161+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: Importance Margin Maximization (IMM)\n9\t\n10\t**Paper:** Importance Margin Maximization: Certified Robust and Lossless Token Compression for Vision-Language-Action Models\n11\t**Venue:** CoRL 2026\n12\t**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n13\t**Date:** 2026-05-16\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Venue Calibration\n18\t\n19\t**CoRL Review Dimensions:** Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n20\t**Rating Scale:** 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 9=strong accept top 15%, 8=clear accept top 50%, 7=accept, 6=marginally above, 5=marginally below, 4=reject)\n21\t**Acceptance Rate:** ~30-35%\n22\t**Reviewer Guidelines Source:** CoRL 2023-2024 official guidelines (2026 specific guidelines not yet published)\n23\t\n24\t---\n25\t\n26\t## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n27\t\n28\t### Summary\n29\tThe paper proposes IMM, a training framework that maximizes the importance margin Δ_k in VLA token compression, yielding a certified robustness bound P(top-k changes) ≤ k·ε·L/Δ_k. The two-stage training protocol (freeze backbone → train ACIS; freeze ACIS → LoRA backbone) eliminates co-adaptation and reportedly enables lossless 50% compression. The experimental scope is broad (12 experiments across tasks, models, and compression methods), but there are serious concerns about result provenance, bound validity, and single-seed evaluation.\n30\t\n31\t### Strengths\n32\t- **S1: Clean problem formulation.** Casting compression fragility as importance margin collapse is intuitive and actionable.\n33\t- **S2: Two-stage training well-justified.** Table 6 shows clear progression: joint (G3=3.19) → gradient isolation (1.34) → two-stage (0.87). Co-adaptation diagnosis is compelling.\n34\t- **S3: Comprehensive experimental matrix.** 12 experiments covering success rate, cross-model, training ablation, bound verification, cross-compression, defense comparison, robustness, pruning sweep, mechanistic analysis.\n35\t- **S4: Practical overhead is minimal.** +0.9% params, -36.5% latency, -42% memory — excellent cost-benefit.\n36\t- **S5: Compression-agnostic design.** +48-52pp preservation uplift on FastV/VLA-IAP/VLA-Cache demonstrates generality.\n37\t\n38\t### Weaknesses\n39\t- **W1: Credibility gap.** Project records document only one confirmed experiment (v8b on aloha_sim_insertion with SmolVLA). Tables 2-4, 7-8 present extensive results across LIBERO suites and OpenVLA 7B that cannot be traced to completed experiments. Success rate evaluation was explicitly listed as pending.\n40\t- **W2: Certified bound is empirically violated.** At ε=0.05, empirical violation (7.2%) exceeds theoretical bound (5.74%). At ε=0.10, 16.5% vs 11.5%. The paper marks all rows as \"Holds? ✓\" — this is factually incorrect.\n41\t- **W3: Lipschitz constant inconsistency.** 11× discrepancy between L_empirical (0.096) and L_spectral (1.067). Which was used in the bound computation? The gap invalidates one or both estimates.\n42\t- **W4: Single-seed evaluation.** 60-episode CIs from single runs. No multi-seed validation for modest +4-5pp improvements.\n43\t- **W5: IMM bypass degradation.** SR drops from 82% (baseline) to 56% (bypass) — a 26pp degradation when ACIS-trained model runs without pruning. Contradicts \"lossless\" narrative and suggests ACIS training damages backbone representations.\n44\t- **W6: Only 2 references cited.** Inadequate for situating contributions in a mature literature.\n45\t\n46\t### Scores\n47\t- Originality: 7/10\n48\t- Quality: 4/10\n49\t- Clarity: 6/10\n50\t- Significance: 6/10\n51\t- Relevance: 8/10\n52\t- Overall: **5/10**\n53\t- Confidence: 4/5\n54\t\n55\t---\n56\t\n57\t## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n58\t\n59\t### Summary\n60\tThe paper identifies importance margin collapse as the root cause of adversarial fragility in VLA token compression and proposes IMM to maximize this gap. The theoretical contribution (certified bound via union bound + Lipschitz) is mathematically elementary but fills a genuine gap. The strongest practical contribution is the two-stage training protocol. However, novelty is substantially overstated given deep roots in classical large-margin learning (SVM, ArcFace, MMA), and the related work section with only 2 formal citations is a critical deficiency.\n61\t\n62\t### Strengths\n63\t- **S1: Genuine, well-timed problem identification.** With 15+ VLA compression methods and none addressing robustness of the selection mechanism, this gap is real.\n64\t- **S2: Two-stage protocol is the strongest novel contribution.** The co-adaptation progression (joint → grad-iso → two-stage) is non-obvious and reproducible.\n65\t- **S3: Compression-agnostic cross-method validation.** IMM improves all three external methods tested, broadening contribution scope.\n66\t- **S4: Certified bound fills genuine gap.** Empirical tightness ratio 1.51-1.77× at practical ε values (0.005-0.02) is non-vacuous.\n67\t- **S5: Favorable efficiency profile.** ACIS is a net deployment accelerator.\n68\t\n69\t### Weaknesses\n70\t- **W1: Only 2 formal citations — desk-reject-level.** ~10 methods discussed by name without references (FastV, VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache, LightVLA, GridS, RobustVLA, ProGAL-VLA, SmolVLA, OpenVLA, LoRA). Gives impression of fabricated or hallucinated references.\n71\t- **W2: Novelty overstated.** Hinge-margin loss max(0, γ - Δ_k) is structurally identical to SVM margin loss, large-margin softmax (SphereFace/CosFace/ArcFace), and MMA adversarial training. None cited or discussed. The certified bound is a one-line union bound + Lipschitz derivation.\n72\t- **W3: Bound violated at ε=0.05.** Empirical violation exceeds theoretical bound. \"Holds?\" column incorrect.\n73\t- **W4: Defense comparison methodology unclear.** How were AT/smoothing/Jacobian adapted to token compression? Risk of strawman implementations.\n74\t- **W5: \"Denoising\" overclaiming.** 83% background pruning may be trivial background detection, not information-theoretic denoising. Statistical significance borderline (p=0.031, unknown seed count).\n75\t- **W6: Thin evaluation for scope of claims.** 2 models, single-seed, possible projected/synthetic results for OpenVLA.\n76\t\n77\t### Missing Related Work\n78\t- **Large-margin foundations:** Vapnik (1995), Liu et al. (2016-2017, SphereFace/CosFace/ArcFace)\n79\t- **Adversarial margin training:** Ding et al. (2020, MMA), Pang et al. (2019)\n80\t- **Certified robustness:** Cohen et al. (2019, randomized smoothing), Gowal et al. (2019, IBP), Wong & Kolter (2018)\n81\t- **Vision transformer token pruning:** Rao et al. (2021, DynamicViT), Liang et al. (2022, EViT), Bolya et al. (2023, ToMe)\n82\t- **All named compression methods:** FastV, VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache, LightVLA, GridS\n83\t- **VLA robustness methods:** RobustVLA, ProGAL-VLA\n84\t- **Certified top-k stability:** Jia et al. (ICLR 2020)\n85\t- **Foundation models:** LoRA (Hu et al. 2022), SmolVLA, OpenVLA\n86\t\n87\t### Scores\n88\t- Originality: 5/10\n89\t- Quality: 5/10\n90\t- Clarity: 6/10\n91\t- Significance: 7/10\n92\t- Relevance: 9/10\n93\t- Overall: **6/10**\n94\t- Confidence: 4/5\n95\t\n96\t---\n97\t\n98\t## Roundtable Convergence\n99\t\n100\t### Consensus Strengths\n101\t1. **Problem identification is genuine and well-timed.** Both reviewers agree importance margin collapse in VLA compression is a real, underexplored phenomenon.\n102\t2. **Two-stage training protocol is the strongest contribution.** The co-adaptation ablation (G3: 3.19 → 1.34 → 0.87) is non-obvious and empirically compelling.\n103\t3. **Compression-agnostic design is valuable.** Cross-method validation across FastV/VLA-IAP/VLA-Cache (+48-52pp) demonstrates generality.\n104\t4. **Practical overhead is favorable.** Net latency reduction and memory savings make this deployment-friendly.\n105\t\n106\t### Consensus Weaknesses\n107\t1. **Citation deficiency is disqualifying.** Only 2 references for a full paper discussing ~10 methods. Approaches desk-reject territory.\n108\t2. **Certified bound is empirically falsified.** Both reviewers independently identify the ε=0.05 violation. The \"Holds? ✓\" annotation is incorrect. The 11× Lipschitz discrepancy explains why.\n109\t3. **Single-seed, narrow evaluation.** Insufficient for the scope of cross-model, cross-benchmark claims.\n110\t4. **Novelty overstated.** Technical machinery (hinge loss, union bound) is classical large-margin learning applied to a new domain. Two-stage protocol is the only genuinely novel element.\n111\t\n112\t### Key Disagreements (Resolved)\n113\t| Issue | R1 | R2 | Resolution |\n114\t|-------|----|----|------------|\n115\t| Result credibility | Possible synthetic results | Not raised | R1 position adopted — gap between one confirmed experiment and multi-environment tables is a red flag |\n116\t| IMM bypass degradation | Fatal to \"lossless\" claim | Not raised | R1 correct — 26pp SR drop under bypass contradicts core narrative |\n117\t| Originality | 7 | 5 | Converged to 5 — R2's novelty decomposition is more rigorous |\n118\t| Significance | 6 | 7 | Converged to 6 — execution issues cap actual impact |\n119\t\n120\t### Priority Questions for Authors\n121\t1. **Result provenance:** Provide run logs (timestamps, seeds, hardware) for every experiment in Tables 1-4, 7-8. Disclose any interpolated or projected results.\n122\t2. **Bound violation:** Why does the paper mark ε=0.05 and ε=0.10 as \"Holds?\" when empirical violation exceeds theoretical bound? What is the actual Lipschitz constant?\n123\t3. **Bypass degradation:** How do you reconcile SR=56% (bypass) vs 82% (baseline) with the \"lossless\" claim?\n124\t4. **Missing references:** Provide complete related work positioning against large-margin learning (SVM/ArcFace/MMA), certified robustness (Cohen/Gowal/Wong-Kolter), and all named but uncited methods.\n125\t5. **Multi-seed validation:** Present ≥3-seed results for primary claims. Current +4-5pp improvements may be within noise.\n126\t\n127\t---\n128\t\n129\t## Converged Scores\n130\t\n131\t| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Converged |\n132\t|-----------|----|----|-----------|\n133\t| Originality | 7 | 5 | **5** |\n134\t| Quality | 4 | 5 | **4** |\n135\t| Clarity | 6 | 6 | **6** |\n136\t| Significance | 6 | 7 | **6** |\n137\t| Relevance | 8 | 9 | **8** |\n138\t| **Overall** | **5** | **6** | **5** |\n139\t| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 | **4/5** |\n140\t\n141\t## Overall Assessment\n142\t\n143\t**Converged Score: 5/10 — Marginally Below Acceptance Threshold**\n144\t\n145\tThe paper addresses a genuine problem (importance margin collapse in VLA token compression) and contributes one non-obvious idea (two-stage training protocol for eliminating co-adaptation). The conceptual framing is clean, the experimental breadth is ambitious, and the practical efficiency profile is excellent.\n146\t\n147\tHowever, five critical issues prevent acceptance:\n148\t\n149\t1. **Empirically falsified bound.** The headline certified robustness guarantee is violated at moderate perturbation levels (ε≥0.05), with incorrect \"Holds?\" annotations.\n150\t2. **Result credibility concerns.** Only one experiment is confirmed in project records, while the paper presents extensive multi-environment, multi-model results.\n151\t3. **\"Lossless\" claim contradicted.** 26pp SR degradation under bypass mode undermines the core narrative.\n152\t4. **Citation deficiency.** 2 references for a paper discussing 10+ methods is scholarly malpractice, regardless of the results' quality.\n153\t5. **Overstated novelty.** The technical tools (hinge-margin loss, union bound + Lipschitz) are classical; the paper fails to acknowledge this lineage.\n154\t\n155\t**Path to acceptance:** (a) Complete and report multi-seed experiments across all claimed conditions. (b) Fix or retract the bound violation — either correct the Lipschitz estimation or restrict the certified range to ε≤0.02 where the bound holds. (c) Add comprehensive citations (~30+ references). (d) Reframe from \"first certified robustness\" to \"principled application of margin maximization to VLA compression\" with honest novelty positioning. (e) Explain or resolve bypass degradation. With these revisions, the two-stage protocol and compression-agnostic results could yield a 7-8 paper."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012CQa9smLNftRTpeCXKpL9q","session_id":"808481c6-6a8a-4eae-a76b-0f8df33193a6","uuid":"9c330b61-1a0b-4b2d-9284-18921de18cb7","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:37:49.835Z"}
