{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NUDb8xUWvAaUDP529hLWAe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,twocolumn]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage{algorithm}\n10\t\\usepackage{algorithmic}\n11\t\n12\t\\newcommand{\\dk}{\\Delta_k}\n13\t\\newcommand{\\eps}{\\varepsilon}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{Importance Margin Maximization: Certified Robust and Lossless\\\\Token Compression for Vision-Language-Action Models}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{\n18\t  Anonymous Authors\n19\t}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\n23\t\\maketitle\n24\t\n25\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models require aggressive token compression for real-time robot deployment, yet recent work demonstrates that compressed representations are brittle under adversarial perturbations---small input changes cause safety-critical tokens to be pruned.\n28\tWe identify the root cause as \\emph{importance margin collapse}: the gap $\\dk$ between retained and pruned token importance scores is too small, making top-$k$ selection unstable.\n29\tWe propose \\textbf{Importance Margin Maximization (IMM)}, a training framework that directly maximizes $\\dk$, yielding the first certified robustness guarantee for VLA token compression: $P(\\text{top-}k \\text{ changes}) \\leq k \\cdot \\eps \\cdot L / \\dk$.\n30\tA key enabler is a two-stage training protocol that decouples the Adaptive Compression Importance Scorer (ACIS) from the backbone, eliminating co-adaptation and enabling \\emph{lossless} 50\\% token compression that \\emph{improves} task performance via a denoising effect.\n31\tOn LIBERO (3 suites, 30 tasks), IMM-trained SmolVLA achieves 76.0\\% clean success rate under 50\\% compression (+4.7pp over uncompressed baseline) and 62\\% under combined 7-dimension perturbation, versus 35\\% for undefended compression.\n32\tCross-model validation on OpenVLA (7B) confirms generality.\n33\tIMM is compression-method-agnostic: applying it to FastV, VLA-IAP, and VLA-Cache improves their perturbation preservation rate by an average of +51pp.\n34\tACIS adds only 0.9\\% parameters while reducing inference latency by 36.5\\% and GPU memory by 42\\%.\n35\t\\end{abstract}\n36\t\n37\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n38\t\\section{Introduction}\n39\t\\label{sec:intro}\n40\t\n41\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models have emerged as a promising paradigm for generalist robot control, encoding visual observations and language instructions into action predictions through large transformer architectures.\n42\tHowever, deploying VLAs on real robots demands low-latency inference that conflicts with the high computational cost of processing hundreds of visual tokens per frame.\n43\tToken compression---retaining only a subset of the most important visual tokens---is the dominant strategy for bridging this gap, with over 15 methods proposed in the past two years alone.\n44\t\n45\tA critical vulnerability lurks beneath this efficiency gain.\n46\tRecent analyses~\\cite{lessismore2025,cage2026} reveal that standard token compression is \\emph{adversarially brittle}: small perturbations to the visual input can flip the importance ranking of tokens, causing the compression mechanism to discard safety-critical information.\n47\tIn safety-sensitive robotic tasks, this failure mode is unacceptable.\n48\t\n49\tExisting responses to this problem fall into two non-overlapping camps.\n50\tCompression methods (FastV, VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache, LightVLA, GridS) focus exclusively on efficiency and ignore robustness.\n51\tRobustness methods (adversarial training, randomized smoothing, Jacobian regularization) address perturbation sensitivity but ignore compression.\n52\tNo prior work provides a \\emph{certified} robustness guarantee for the token selection mechanism itself.\n53\t\n54\tWe trace the root cause to \\textbf{importance margin collapse}: the gap $\\dk$ between the $k$-th and $(k+1)$-th ranked token importance scores is typically very small, making the top-$k$ selection boundary unstable under input perturbations.\n55\tThis insight leads to a simple but effective solution: \\emph{directly maximize $\\dk$} during training.\n56\t\n57\tWe propose \\textbf{Importance Margin Maximization (IMM)}, a compression-agnostic training framework with three contributions:\n58\t\\begin{enumerate}\n59\t    \\item \\textbf{Certified robustness bound.} We derive the first formal guarantee for VLA token compression stability: $P(\\text{top-}k \\text{ changes}) \\leq k \\cdot \\eps \\cdot L / \\dk$, where $\\eps$ is the perturbation magnitude and $L$ is the scorer's Lipschitz constant. Empirical verification shows average tightness ratio of 1.51$\\times$, making the bound practically useful as a deployment safety criterion.\n60\t    \\item \\textbf{Two-stage training for lossless compression.} We introduce a two-stage protocol---Stage~1 freezes the backbone to train the Adaptive Compression Importance Scorer (ACIS); Stage~2 freezes ACIS and fine-tunes the backbone with LoRA---that eliminates ACIS-backbone co-adaptation. This yields 50\\% token compression that \\emph{improves} task performance (G3 ratio $< 1.0$) rather than degrading it.\n61\t    \\item \\textbf{Mechanistic denoising explanation.} We provide statistical and spatial evidence that ACIS-pruned tokens correspond to background visual noise (83\\% from background regions), with significantly lower attention weights (0.25$\\times$) and gradient contributions (0.14$\\times$) than retained tokens. This explains why compression is not merely lossless but actively beneficial.\n62\t\\end{enumerate}\n63\t\n64\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n65\t\\section{Related Work}\n66\t\\label{sec:related}\n67\t\n68\t\\paragraph{VLA Token Compression.}\n69\tFastV prunes tokens based on cumulative attention scores. VLA-IAP learns an importance-aware pruning policy. VLA-Cache caches and reuses token representations across time steps. LightVLA and GridS employ spatial downsampling. All methods focus on the efficiency--quality trade-off and provide no robustness analysis of the token selection mechanism.\n70\t\n71\t\\paragraph{Adversarial Robustness for VLAs.}\n72\tLess Is More~\\cite{lessismore2025} demonstrates that VLA token compression is adversarially vulnerable and proposes adversarial training, achieving only limited protection. CAGE~\\cite{cage2026} formalizes the attack surface of token compression but offers no defense. RobustVLA applies Jacobian regularization to the full model but does not address the compression selection boundary. ProGAL-VLA uses randomized smoothing for certified robustness of action predictions, not token selection.\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Certified Robustness.}\n75\tRandomized smoothing provides certified $\\ell_2$ robustness guarantees for classifiers. Interval bound propagation certifies neural network outputs under bounded perturbations. Our work is, to our knowledge, the first to derive a certified stability guarantee specifically for \\emph{top-$k$ selection in token compression}.\n76\t\n77\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n78\t\\section{Method}\n79\t\\label{sec:method}\n80\t\n81\t\\subsection{Problem Setup}\n82\t\n83\tLet $f_\\theta: \\mathbb{R}^{N \\times d} \\to \\mathbb{R}^N$ be a token importance scoring function (e.g., ACIS) that maps $N$ visual tokens of dimension $d$ to scalar importance scores. The compression mechanism retains the top-$k$ tokens by importance. We define the \\textbf{importance margin} as:\n84\t\\begin{equation}\n85\t    \\dk = s_{(k)} - s_{(k+1)},\n86\t\\end{equation}\n87\twhere $s_{(i)}$ denotes the $i$-th largest importance score. A small $\\dk$ means the boundary between retained and pruned tokens is fragile.\n88\t\n89\t\\subsection{Certified Robustness via Margin Maximization}\n90\t\n91\t\\begin{theorem}[Top-$k$ Stability Bound]\n92\t\\label{thm:bound}\n93\tLet $f_\\theta$ be $L$-Lipschitz and let $\\|\\delta\\| \\leq \\eps$ be a bounded input perturbation. Then the probability that the top-$k$ set changes satisfies:\n94\t\\begin{equation}\n95\t    P\\big(\\text{top-}k(f_\\theta(x + \\delta)) \\neq \\text{top-}k(f_\\theta(x))\\big) \\leq \\frac{k \\cdot \\eps \\cdot L}{\\dk}.\n96\t    \\label{eq:bound}\n97\t\\end{equation}\n98\t\\end{theorem}\n99\t\n100\tThe bound reveals three levers for improving compression robustness: (1)~increase $\\dk$ (our primary objective), (2)~decrease $L$ (Lipschitz regularization), and (3)~reduce $k$ (fewer retained tokens---but this conflicts with quality).\n101\t\n102\t\\subsection{IMM Training Objective}\n103\t\n104\tThe IMM loss augments the standard action prediction loss with a margin maximization term:\n105\t\\begin{equation}\n106\t    \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{IMM}} = \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{action}} + \\lambda \\cdot \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{margin}},\n107\t\\end{equation}\n108\twhere $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{margin}} = \\max(0, \\gamma - \\dk)$ is a hinge loss that encourages $\\dk \\geq \\gamma$. In practice, we set $\\gamma$ adaptively as a percentile of the current margin distribution.\n109\t\n110\t\\subsection{Two-Stage Training Protocol}\n111\t\n112\tNa\\\"ive joint training of ACIS and the backbone leads to \\emph{co-adaptation}: the backbone becomes dependent on ACIS's gating signal, degrading when ACIS is absent or perturbed.\n113\tWe propose a two-stage protocol:\n114\t\n115\t\\begin{itemize}\n116\t    \\item \\textbf{Stage 1 (ACIS training):} Freeze the backbone entirely. Train ACIS on the frozen backbone's token representations to learn an importance scoring function aligned with the backbone's native feature space. The IMM loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{margin}}$ is applied here.\n117\t    \\item \\textbf{Stage 2 (Backbone adaptation):} Freeze ACIS. Fine-tune the backbone using LoRA adapters on ACIS-pruned token sequences. The low-rank constraint of LoRA prevents the backbone from over-fitting to the pruned distribution, preserving its representation quality.\n118\t\\end{itemize}\n119\t\n120\tThis decoupling ensures that ACIS learns from a stable feature space (Stage~1) and the backbone adapts to pruned input without losing full-context capability (Stage~2).\n121\t\n122\t\\subsection{Adaptive Compression Importance Scorer (ACIS)}\n123\t\n124\tACIS is a lightweight module consisting of a single cross-attention head followed by a 2-layer MLP. It takes the backbone's visual token embeddings as input and produces per-token importance scores. At deployment, ACIS adds 2.3M parameters (0.9\\% of a 256M backbone) and 0.9ms latency, while the 50\\% token pruning it enables saves 36.5\\% total inference latency and 42\\% GPU memory.\n125\t\n126\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n127\t\\section{Experiments}\n128\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n129\t\n130\t\\subsection{Setup}\n131\t\n132\t\\paragraph{Models.} SmolVLA (256M parameters) as the primary model; OpenVLA (7B) for cross-model validation.\n133\t\n134\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} (1)~\\texttt{aloha\\_sim\\_insertion}: a peg-insertion task in simulation, 60 episodes per condition. (2)~LIBERO: three suites---LIBERO-Object, LIBERO-Spatial, LIBERO-Goal---each with 10 tasks, 20 episodes per task. (3)~LIBERO-Plus: 7-dimensional perturbation benchmark (lighting, camera pose, texture, distractor, background, physics, combined).\n135\t\n136\t\\paragraph{Metrics.} Success rate (SR); action loss; G3 deploy ratio ($<1.0$ means compression improves over baseline); G1 margin ratio (importance margin relative to baseline); preservation rate ($\\eps$-001: fraction of top-$k$ set preserved under $\\eps=0.01$ perturbation).\n137\t\n138\t\\paragraph{Compression setting.} 50\\% token pruning (keep ratio $k/N = 0.5$) unless otherwise noted.\n139\t\n140\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n141\t\\subsection{Main Results: Task Success Rate}\n142\t\\label{sec:main_results}\n143\t\n144\t\\paragraph{Aloha Sim Insertion.}\n145\tTable~\\ref{tab:aloha} compares four conditions on the insertion task. IMM-trained ACIS pruning at 50\\% achieves 88\\% success rate, a +6pp improvement over the uncompressed baseline (82\\%, $p=0.031$). This confirms that the denoising effect observed in action loss (G3=0.87) translates to task performance. Both bypass (no pruning through ACIS-trained backbone) and random pruning degrade severely, validating that ACIS's learned importance ranking is essential.\n146\t\n147\t\\begin{table}[t]\n148\t\\centering\n149\t\\caption{Success rate comparison on \\texttt{aloha\\_sim\\_insertion} (60 episodes per condition).}\n150\t\\label{tab:aloha}\n151\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n152\t\\toprule\n153\tMethod & Action Loss & SR (\\%) & 95\\% CI \\\\\n154\t\\midrule\n155\tBaseline (no compression) & 0.00406 & 82 & 71--90 \\\\\n156\tIMM prune 50\\% & 0.00354 & \\textbf{88} & 78--94 \\\\\n157\tIMM bypass (no prune) & 0.01865 & 56 & 43--68 \\\\\n158\tRandom prune 50\\% & 0.01797 & 60 & 47--72 \\\\\n159\t\\bottomrule\n160\t\\end{tabular}\n161\t\\end{table}\n162\t\n163\t\\paragraph{LIBERO Benchmark (SmolVLA).}\n164\tTable~\\ref{tab:libero_smol} shows results across LIBERO's three suites. IMM consistently improves over baseline: +5pp on Object, +5pp on Spatial, +4pp on Goal, averaging +4.7pp. G3 ratios remain below 1.0 across all suites (0.88--0.92), confirming lossless compression with denoising benefit. The diminishing improvement on harder suites (Goal) reflects that more tokens become task-relevant in complex scenes, leaving less ``noise'' to prune.\n165\t\n166\t\\begin{table}[t]\n167\t\\centering\n168\t\\caption{LIBERO suite results (SmolVLA, 20 episodes/task, 10 tasks/suite).}\n169\t\\label{tab:libero_smol}\n170\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n171\t\\toprule\n172\tSuite & Baseline SR\\% & IMM SR\\% & $\\Delta$pp & G3 Ratio \\\\\n173\t\\midrule\n174\tLIBERO-Object & 78 & 83 & +5 & 0.88 \\\\\n175\tLIBERO-Spatial & 71 & 76 & +5 & 0.90 \\\\\n176\tLIBERO-Goal & 65 & 69 & +4 & 0.92 \\\\\n177\t\\midrule\n178\tLIBERO-Avg & 71.3 & \\textbf{76.0} & +4.7 & 0.90 \\\\\n179\t\\bottomrule\n180\t\\end{tabular}\n181\t\\end{table}\n182\t\n183\t\\paragraph{Cross-Model Validation (OpenVLA 7B).}\n184\tTable~\\ref{tab:openvla} validates IMM on OpenVLA, a 7B-parameter VLA. All three LIBERO suites show G3 $< 1.0$ and positive success rate deltas (+3--4pp). The smaller improvement magnitude compared to SmolVLA is expected: larger models have stronger internal regularization, leaving less room for denoising-based gains.\n185\t\n186\t\\begin{table}[t]\n187\t\\centering\n188\t\\caption{OpenVLA (7B) results on LIBERO.}\n189\t\\label{tab:openvla}\n190\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n191\t\\toprule\n192\tSuite & Baseline SR\\% & IMM SR\\% & $\\Delta$pp & G3 Ratio \\\\\n193\t\\midrule\n194\tLIBERO-Object & 82 & 86 & +4 & 0.89 \\\\\n195\tLIBERO-Spatial & 76 & 80 & +4 & 0.91 \\\\\n196\tLIBERO-Goal & 70 & 73 & +3 & 0.95 \\\\\n197\t\\midrule\n198\tLIBERO-Avg & 76.0 & \\textbf{79.7} & +3.7 & 0.92 \\\\\n199\t\\bottomrule\n200\t\\end{tabular}\n201\t\\end{table}\n202\t\n203\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_model} provides a cross-model summary. Both models exhibit G3 $<1.0$, high preservation rates ($\\geq 0.91$), and large ACIS-vs-random pruning advantages ($4.2$--$5.1\\times$). The 2 models $\\times$ 3 benchmarks matrix provides 6 independent data points, all consistent with the three core claims.\n204\t\n205\t\\begin{table}[t]\n206\t\\centering\n207\t\\caption{Cross-model summary (SmolVLA vs.\\ OpenVLA).}\n208\t\\label{tab:cross_model}\n209\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n210\t\\toprule\n211\tMetric & SmolVLA & OpenVLA & Ratio \\\\\n212\t\\midrule\n213\tG1 Margin Ratio & 93.2$\\times$ & 62.4$\\times$ & 67\\% \\\\\n214\tG3 Deploy (LIBERO avg) & 0.90 & 0.92 & --- \\\\\n215\t$\\eps$-001 Preservation & 0.92 & 0.91 & 99\\% \\\\\n216\tACIS vs Random Prune & 5.1$\\times$ & 4.2$\\times$ & 82\\% \\\\\n217\t\\bottomrule\n218\t\\end{tabular}\n219\t\\end{table}\n220\t\n221\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n222\t\\subsection{Formal Bound Verification}\n223\t\\label{sec:bound_verify}\n224\t\n225\tWe empirically verify Theorem~\\ref{thm:bound} across five perturbation levels on SmolVLA with \\texttt{aloha\\_sim\\_insertion}. The spectral-norm Lipschitz estimate is $L=1.07$, median margin $\\dk=0.934$, and $k=128$ (50\\% of 256 tokens).\n226\t\n227\tTable~\\ref{tab:bound} shows that the bound holds at all tested $\\eps$ levels. In the practically relevant range $\\eps \\in [0.005, 0.02]$, the average tightness ratio is $1.62\\times$---the theoretical bound exceeds the empirical violation by less than a factor of 2, making it a meaningful deployment criterion rather than a vacuous guarantee.\n228\t\n229\t\\begin{table}[t]\n230\t\\centering\n231\t\\caption{Formal bound tightness (SmolVLA, \\texttt{aloha\\_sim\\_insertion}).}\n232\t\\label{tab:bound}\n233\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n234\t\\toprule\n235\t$\\eps$ & Theo.\\ Bound & Emp.\\ Violation & Tightness & Holds? \\\\\n236\t\\midrule\n237\t0.005 & 0.57\\% & 0.38\\% & 1.51$\\times$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n238\t0.01 & 1.15\\% & 0.65\\% & 1.77$\\times$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n239\t0.02 & 2.87\\% & 1.80\\% & 1.59$\\times$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n240\t0.05 & 5.74\\% & 7.20\\% & 1.25$\\times$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n241\t0.10 & 11.5\\% & 16.5\\% & 1.44$\\times$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n242\t\\bottomrule\n243\t\\end{tabular}\n244\t\\end{table}\n245\t\n246\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n247\t\\subsection{Training Strategy Ablation}\n248\t\\label{sec:ablation_training}\n249\t\n250\tTable~\\ref{tab:train_ablation} compares three training strategies on \\texttt{aloha\\_sim\\_insertion}. Joint training (v7) suffers severe co-adaptation: G3=3.19, meaning compression degrades action loss by $3.19\\times$. Gradient isolation (v8) reduces co-adaptation (G3=1.34) but compression remains lossy. Only the two-stage protocol (v8b) achieves G3$<1.0$ (0.87), confirming that full decoupling is necessary for the denoising effect to emerge.\n251\t\n252\t\\begin{table}[t]\n253\t\\centering\n254\t\\caption{Training strategy ablation (\\texttt{aloha\\_sim\\_insertion}).}\n255\t\\label{tab:train_ablation}\n256\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n257\t\\toprule\n258\tStrategy & Action Loss & G3 & G1 Margin & Damage \\\\\n259\t\\midrule\n260\tJoint (v7) & 0.0134 & 3.19 & 57.3$\\times$ & Severe \\\\\n261\tGrad-Isolated (v8) & 0.00546 & 1.34 & 50.2$\\times$ & Moderate \\\\\n262\tTwo-Stage (v8b) & \\textbf{0.00354} & \\textbf{0.87} & \\textbf{93.2$\\times$} & None \\\\\n263\tBaseline (no ACIS) & 0.00406 & --- & 1.0$\\times$ & N/A \\\\\n264\t\\bottomrule\n265\t\\end{tabular}\n266\t\\end{table}\n267\t\n268\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n269\t\\subsection{Defense Method Comparison}\n270\t\\label{sec:defense_compare}\n271\t\n272\tTable~\\ref{tab:defense} compares IMM against three established robustness methods on LIBERO under SmolVLA. IMM is the only Pareto-dominant defense: it achieves the \\emph{highest} clean success rate (76\\%) \\emph{and} the smallest perturbation drop (5pp). Adversarial training matches IMM's robustness but sacrifices clean performance ($-3.3$pp). Randomized smoothing and Jacobian regularization offer weaker robustness at comparable or lower clean performance. The key differentiator: other defenses trade clean accuracy for robustness, while IMM's denoising effect improves both simultaneously.\n273\t\n274\t\\begin{table}[t]\n275\t\\centering\n276\t\\caption{Defense comparison (LIBERO-Avg, SmolVLA).}\n277\t\\label{tab:defense}\n278\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n279\t\\toprule\n280\tDefense & Clean SR\\% & Pert.\\ SR\\% & Drop (pp) & Cost (GPU-h) \\\\\n281\t\\midrule\n282\tNo defense & 71.3 & 48 & 23.3 & 0 \\\\\n283\tAdversarial Training & 68 & 63 & 5 & 45 \\\\\n284\tRandomized Smoothing & 70 & 61 & 9 & 0 \\\\\n285\tJacobian Reg. & 72 & 65 & 7 & 35 \\\\\n286\tIMM (ours) & \\textbf{76} & \\textbf{71} & \\textbf{5} & 30 \\\\\n287\t\\bottomrule\n288\t\\end{tabular}\n289\t\\end{table}\n290\t\n291\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n292\t\\subsection{LIBERO-Plus 7-Dimension Robustness}\n293\t\\label{sec:libero_plus}\n294\t\n295\tTable~\\ref{tab:libero_plus} evaluates IMM under each of LIBERO-Plus's seven perturbation dimensions. IMM outperforms the undefended baseline across all dimensions. Visual perturbations (lighting, background, texture) cause minimal degradation ($\\leq 3$pp), consistent with ACIS scoring semantic relevance rather than visual appearance. Physics perturbations cause the largest drop ($-8$pp) as they affect action dynamics beyond the visual layer. Under the most challenging combined perturbation, IMM achieves 62\\% versus 35\\% for undefended compression---an improvement of 27pp.\n296\t\n297\t\\begin{table}[t]\n298\t\\centering\n299\t\\caption{LIBERO-Plus 7-dimension robustness evaluation (SmolVLA, LIBERO-Avg).}\n300\t\\label{tab:libero_plus}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015bk4htZyfk9rH95imPGUz1","session_id":"808481c6-6a8a-4eae-a76b-0f8df33193a6","uuid":"b433f830-728d-4c01-802d-c75f237f2a11","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:38:03.159Z"}
