{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013mgHVBf7pVDCdqxT43MMfc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HqfuUp8eVm4Gyyxvm8kTe6","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T075046/best/paper.tex","offset":300,"limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6931,"cache_read_input_tokens":27353,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6931,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":34,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015bk4htZyfk9rH95imPGUz1","session_id":"808481c6-6a8a-4eae-a76b-0f8df33193a6","uuid":"98639f19-22d8-4c51-8797-816cdc5ab629"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HqfuUp8eVm4Gyyxvm8kTe6","type":"tool_result","content":"300\t\\label{tab:libero_plus}\n301\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n302\t\\toprule\n303\tPerturbation & IMM SR\\% & No Def.\\ SR\\% & IMM $\\Delta$pp & No Def.\\ $\\Delta$pp \\\\\n304\t\\midrule\n305\tClean & 76 & 71.3 & --- & --- \\\\\n306\tLighting & 73 & 58 & $-3$ & $-13$ \\\\\n307\tCamera Pose & 70 & 52 & $-6$ & $-19$ \\\\\n308\tTexture & 74 & 60 & $-2$ & $-11$ \\\\\n309\tDistractor & 72 & 55 & $-4$ & $-16$ \\\\\n310\tBackground & 75 & 62 & $-1$ & $-9$ \\\\\n311\tPhysics & 68 & 46 & $-8$ & $-25$ \\\\\n312\tCombined (all 7) & \\textbf{62} & 35 & $-14$ & $-36$ \\\\\n313\t\\bottomrule\n314\t\\end{tabular}\n315\t\\end{table}\n316\t\n317\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n318\t\\subsection{Cross-Compression Compatibility}\n319\t\\label{sec:cross_compress}\n320\t\n321\tIMM is not tied to ACIS as the compression mechanism. Table~\\ref{tab:cross_compress} shows that applying IMM training to three external compression methods yields consistent improvements: +48--52pp preservation rate uplift and 17\\% average action loss reduction. This confirms that IMM's margin maximization reshapes the importance landscape in a way that benefits any reasonable top-$k$ selection strategy.\n322\t\n323\t\\begin{table}[t]\n324\t\\centering\n325\t\\caption{Cross-compression compatibility (\\texttt{aloha\\_sim\\_insertion}, SmolVLA).}\n326\t\\label{tab:cross_compress}\n327\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n328\t\\toprule\n329\tMethod & Loss (base) & Loss (+IMM) & Pres.\\ (base) & Pres.\\ (+IMM) \\\\\n330\t\\midrule\n331\tFastV & 0.0058 & 0.0048 & 41\\% & 89\\% \\\\\n332\tVLA-IAP & 0.0052 & 0.0044 & 38\\% & 90\\% \\\\\n333\tVLA-Cache & 0.0063 & 0.0051 & 35\\% & 87\\% \\\\\n334\tACIS (ours) & 0.0041 & 0.0035 & 34\\% & 92\\% \\\\\n335\t\\bottomrule\n336\t\\end{tabular}\n337\t\\end{table}\n338\t\n339\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n340\t\\subsection{Pruning Ratio Sweep}\n341\t\\label{sec:ratio_sweep}\n342\t\n343\tFigure~\\ref{fig:ratio_sweep} (data in Table~\\ref{tab:ratio_sweep}) shows G3 and success rate as a function of keep ratio. The U-shaped G3 curve confirms the denoising mechanism: a sweet spot exists at 0.4--0.6 where pruned tokens are predominantly noise. At keep ratio 0.55, G3 reaches its minimum (0.83) and success rate peaks at 90\\%. Below 0.4, excessive pruning removes task-critical tokens (G3 $>1.0$). Above 0.7, insufficient pruning retains noise, diminishing the denoising benefit.\n344\t\n345\t\\begin{table}[t]\n346\t\\centering\n347\t\\caption{Pruning ratio sweep (SmolVLA, \\texttt{aloha\\_sim\\_insertion}).}\n348\t\\label{tab:ratio_sweep}\n349\t\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n350\t\\toprule\n351\tKeep Ratio & G3 Ratio & Action Loss & SR\\% \\\\\n352\t\\midrule\n353\t0.30 & 1.18 & 0.00479 & 74 \\\\\n354\t0.40 & 0.91 & 0.00370 & 85 \\\\\n355\t0.50 & 0.87 & 0.00354 & 88 \\\\\n356\t0.55 & \\textbf{0.83} & \\textbf{0.00338} & \\textbf{90} \\\\\n357\t0.60 & 0.84 & 0.00342 & 90 \\\\\n358\t0.70 & 0.86 & 0.00350 & 87 \\\\\n359\t0.80 & 0.91 & 0.00370 & 85 \\\\\n360\t0.90 & 0.97 & 0.00394 & 83 \\\\\n361\t\\bottomrule\n362\t\\end{tabular}\n363\t\\end{table}\n364\t\n365\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n366\t\\subsection{Token Importance Mechanistic Analysis}\n367\t\\label{sec:mechanistic}\n368\t\n369\tWe analyze the properties of pruned versus retained tokens to provide mechanistic grounding for the denoising claim.\n370\t\n371\t\\paragraph{Attention and gradient analysis.}\n372\tTable~\\ref{tab:mechanistic} shows that pruned tokens receive $4\\times$ lower attention weight and contribute $7.4\\times$ less gradient magnitude to the action loss than retained tokens (both $p < 0.001$). Pruned tokens collectively account for only 3.2\\% of the total action loss contribution despite representing 50\\% of all tokens.\n373\t\n374\t\\begin{table}[t]\n375\t\\centering\n376\t\\caption{Token importance mechanistic analysis (SmolVLA, \\texttt{aloha\\_sim\\_insertion}).}\n377\t\\label{tab:mechanistic}\n378\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n379\t\\toprule\n380\tMetric & Pruned & Retained & Ratio & $p$-value \\\\\n381\t\\midrule\n382\tAvg Attention Weight & 0.0018 & 0.0073 & 0.247 & $<$0.001 \\\\\n383\tAvg Gradient Magnitude & 0.00012 & 0.00089 & 0.135 & $<$0.001 \\\\\n384\tAction Loss Contribution & 3.2\\% & 96.8\\% & 0.033 & $<$0.001 \\\\\n385\t\\bottomrule\n386\t\\end{tabular}\n387\t\\end{table}\n388\t\n389\t\\paragraph{Spatial distribution.}\n390\tTable~\\ref{tab:spatial} reveals that 83\\% of pruned tokens originate from background regions, compared to only 34\\% of retained tokens. Conversely, target-object and gripper regions---the most action-relevant areas---are heavily represented among retained tokens (38\\% and 16\\%) but rarely pruned (8\\% and 4\\%). ACIS effectively learns automatic background removal.\n391\t\n392\t\\begin{table}[t]\n393\t\\centering\n394\t\\caption{Spatial distribution of pruned vs.\\ retained tokens.}\n395\t\\label{tab:spatial}\n396\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n397\t\\toprule\n398\tRegion & \\% of Pruned & \\% of Retained \\\\\n399\t\\midrule\n400\tBackground & 83 & 34 \\\\\n401\tTable surface & 5 & 12 \\\\\n402\tTarget object & 8 & 38 \\\\\n403\tGripper & 4 & 16 \\\\\n404\t\\bottomrule\n405\t\\end{tabular}\n406\t\\end{table}\n407\t\n408\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n409\t\\subsection{Mask Ratio Annealing Ablation}\n410\t\\label{sec:annealing}\n411\t\n412\tWe test whether curriculum-style annealing (keep ratio $0.9 \\to 0.5$) improves over the fixed-ratio two-stage protocol. Table~\\ref{tab:annealing} shows no significant benefit: annealing achieves G3=0.89 versus 0.87 for fixed ratio, with a slightly lower margin (88.7$\\times$ vs.\\ 93.2$\\times$). Since the two-stage protocol already provides a well-separated importance landscape, curriculum annealing is unnecessary and adds a hyperparameter.\n413\t\n414\t\\begin{table}[t]\n415\t\\centering\n416\t\\caption{Mask ratio annealing vs.\\ fixed ratio.}\n417\t\\label{tab:annealing}\n418\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n419\t\\toprule\n420\tStrategy & Action Loss & G3 & G1 Margin & Extra Hyperparam \\\\\n421\t\\midrule\n422\tTwo-Stage Fixed 0.5 & \\textbf{0.00354} & \\textbf{0.87} & \\textbf{93.2$\\times$} & None \\\\\n423\tAnnealing $0.9 \\to 0.5$ & 0.00362 & 0.89 & 88.7$\\times$ & Schedule \\\\\n424\t\\bottomrule\n425\t\\end{tabular}\n426\t\\end{table}\n427\t\n428\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n429\t\\subsection{ACIS Deployment Overhead}\n430\t\\label{sec:overhead}\n431\t\n432\tTable~\\ref{tab:overhead} quantifies ACIS's deployment cost. The module adds 2.3M parameters (0.9\\% of 256M) and 0.9ms of scoring latency. However, pruning 50\\% of tokens reduces downstream transformer computation, yielding a net latency reduction of 36.5\\% (45.2ms $\\to$ 28.7ms) and 42\\% memory savings (1840MB $\\to$ 1068MB). ACIS is a net deployment accelerator.\n433\t\n434\t\\begin{table}[t]\n435\t\\centering\n436\t\\caption{ACIS deployment overhead (SmolVLA).}\n437\t\\label{tab:overhead}\n438\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n439\t\\toprule\n440\tMetric & Baseline & With ACIS+Prune & Change \\\\\n441\t\\midrule\n442\tParameters (M) & 256 & 258.3 & +0.9\\% \\\\\n443\tFLOPs/token (M) & 12.5 & 12.68 & +1.4\\% \\\\\n444\tInference Latency (ms) & 45.2 & 28.7 & $-$36.5\\% \\\\\n445\tGPU Memory (MB) & 1840 & 1068 & $-$42\\% \\\\\n446\t\\bottomrule\n447\t\\end{tabular}\n448\t\\end{table}\n449\t\n450\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n451\t\\section{Conclusion}\n452\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n453\t\n454\tWe presented Importance Margin Maximization (IMM), the first training framework to provide certified robustness for VLA token compression. By identifying importance margin collapse as the root cause of compression brittleness, we derived a formal bound on top-$k$ selection stability and showed it is empirically tight (average $1.51\\times$). Our two-stage training protocol eliminates ACIS-backbone co-adaptation, enabling 50\\% token compression that improves task performance by 4--6pp through a mechanistically-grounded denoising effect. Cross-model (SmolVLA, OpenVLA) and cross-benchmark (aloha\\_sim\\_insertion, LIBERO) validation on a $2 \\times 2$ matrix of conditions, combined with compatibility across three external compression methods, demonstrates that IMM is a general-purpose solution for safe VLA deployment. ACIS adds negligible overhead (0.9\\% parameters) while reducing inference latency by 36.5\\%.\n455\t\n456\t\\paragraph{Limitations.} Our formal bound relies on the spectral-norm Lipschitz estimate, which loosens at large $\\eps$ ($>0.05$). Physics perturbations, which affect action dynamics rather than visual representations, remain the weakest point of token-level defenses. Extending IMM to temporal token selection in multi-frame settings is future work.\n457\t\n458\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n459\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n460\t\n461\t\\bibitem{lessismore2025}\n462\tLess Is More Until It Breaks: Adversarial Vulnerability of VLA Token Compression.\n463\t\\textit{arXiv:2601.12042}, 2025.\n464\t\n465\t\\bibitem{cage2026}\n466\tCAGE: Certified Adversarial Robustness Guarantees for Token Compression.\n467\t\\textit{Proceedings of ICML}, 2026.\n468\t\n469\t\\end{thebibliography}\n470\t\n471\t\\end{document}\n472\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015bk4htZyfk9rH95imPGUz1","session_id":"808481c6-6a8a-4eae-a76b-0f8df33193a6","uuid":"bf38e691-6f80-43d3-9d28-5e7bb41ff6c8","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:38:09.145Z"}
