{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015bk4htZyfk9rH95imPGUz1","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have sufficient material to write a comprehensive review. Let me produce the review based on my analysis of both paper versions (the \"best\" optimistic scenario and the \"median\" realistic scenario), the project memory, and the paper framing document.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper proposes Importance Margin Maximization (IMM), a training framework that maximizes the gap between retained and pruned token importance scores in VLA token compression, yielding a formal top-k stability bound. A two-stage training protocol (freeze backbone/train ACIS, then freeze ACIS/train LoRA) eliminates co-adaptation and enables near-lossless or beneficial 50% compression. The idea of connecting margin-based robustness to token selection is clean, but the theoretical contribution is shallow and the related work coverage is inadequate for a venue submission.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Clear problem formulation.** The identification of \"importance margin collapse\" as the root cause of compression brittleness is intuitive and well-motivated. The connection between margin magnitude and selection stability is easy to communicate and operationalize.\n\n2. **Compression-agnostic framing.** Demonstrating that IMM training improves robustness of *external* compression methods (FastV, VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache) by +53pp is a strong result that elevates IMM from \"yet another compression method\" to a general training strategy. This is the paper's most novel positioning choice.\n\n3. **Two-stage training insight is practically useful.** The ablation showing G3 degrades from 0.87 (two-stage) to 3.29 (joint) is a compelling engineering contribution. The explanation via co-adaptation is mechanistically clear and the fix is simple to implement.\n\n4. **Strong experimental coverage for a 6-page paper.** Cross-model (SmolVLA + OpenVLA), cross-benchmark (aloha_sim + LIBERO), cross-compression (3 external methods), and multi-perturbation (7 dimensions) validation provides reasonable breadth.\n\n5. **Denoising effect is a surprising and interesting finding.** Compression improving task performance (G3 < 1.0) is counterintuitive and the spatial analysis (83% pruned tokens from background) provides mechanistic grounding.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**Major:**\n\n1. **Proposition 1 is theoretically trivial.** The bound P(top-k flip) <= k * eps * L / Delta_k is a direct application of Lipschitz continuity to pairwise score differences with a union bound over k positions. This is not a \"certified robustness guarantee\" in the sense used by the certified robustness community (Cohen et al., Lecuyer et al.), which provide guarantees under *any* attack within the threat model. The paper's bound is a probabilistic upper bound on a deterministic event---if the scorer is deterministic and Lipschitz, the top-k set either changes or it does not; the bound should be stated as a sufficient condition (Delta_k > k * eps * L implies no flip), not a probability. The probabilistic framing appears to conflate uncertainty from data distribution with adversarial worst-case. This weakens the \"certified\" claim substantially.\n\n2. **Massive gap in vision transformer token pruning literature.** The paper cites zero works from the extensive ViT token pruning/merging literature: ToMe (Bolya et al., ICLR 2023), DynamicViT (Rao et al., NeurIPS 2021), EViT (Liang et al., ICLR 2022), ATS (Fayyaz et al., NeurIPS 2022), SPViT (Kong et al., ECCV 2022), AdaViT (Meng et al., ICLR 2022). These methods face identical importance-margin issues. DynamicViT in particular learns token importance scores via a prediction module---architecturally very similar to ACIS. Not engaging with this literature is a serious omission.\n\n3. **Margin-based methods have deep history not acknowledged.** The idea of maximizing margins for robustness is foundational to machine learning (SVMs, large-margin classifiers, contrastive learning). More directly relevant: margin-based certified defenses in the adversarial robustness literature (Elsayed et al., \"Large Margin Deep Networks for Classification,\" NeurIPS 2018; Ding et al., \"MMA Training,\" NeurIPS 2020). The paper does not discuss how its margin concept relates to or differs from these. The novelty claim of \"first to maximize margins for robustness\" is unsupported without this contextualization.\n\n4. **Differentiation from CAGE [7] is unclear.** The paper states CAGE \"formalizes the attack surface of token compression but offers no defense.\" However, CAGE also provides certified robustness guarantees for compression (hence \"Certified\" in its title). The paper must explain precisely how IMM's guarantee differs from CAGE's---is it tighter? Does it require fewer assumptions? Does it cover a different threat model? Without this, the novelty over CAGE is ambiguous.\n\n5. **\"First robustness-aware framework\" claim is overstated.** The paper acknowledges RobustVLA (Jacobian regularization) and adversarial training as existing defenses, then claims to be \"first\" by restricting scope to \"certified\" + \"token compression.\" This is a very narrow novelty wedge, especially given CAGE's existence.\n\n**Minor:**\n\n6. **Only 2 references in the bibliography of the best-case paper (lessismore2025, cage2026).** Even the median version has only ~10 references. For a venue submission, this is unacceptable. A 6-page paper at CoRL should still have 25-40 references.\n\n7. **No comparison with token merging approaches.** ToMe-style methods that merge tokens rather than pruning them may be inherently more robust (merged tokens retain information from both). This alternative is never discussed.\n\n8. **The Lipschitz constant estimation method is not described.** The paper reports L=1.07 (spectral norm estimate) but does not explain how this is computed or whether it is a tight estimate. Loose L estimates make the bound vacuous.\n\n9. **Tightness ratio inconsistency.** The best-case paper claims 1.51x average tightness while the median version claims 6.2x. A 6.2x tightness ratio means the bound overestimates the actual flip probability by 6x---still informative directionally but far from \"tight\" in the certified robustness sense.\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\n1. **Vision Transformer token pruning/merging**: ToMe (Bolya et al., 2023), DynamicViT (Rao et al., 2021), EViT (Liang et al., 2022), ATS (Fayyaz et al., 2022), A-ViT (Yin et al., 2022). ACIS is architecturally similar to DynamicViT's prediction module.\n\n2. **Large-margin deep learning**: Elsayed et al. (2018), Ding et al. (2020, MMA Training), Sun et al. (2015, LMCL). These directly maximize classification margins for robustness---the conceptual ancestor of IMM.\n\n3. **Certified robustness beyond randomized smoothing**: Interval Bound Propagation (Gowal et al., 2018), CROWN (Zhang et al., 2018), Lipschitz-based certificates (Tsuzuku et al., 2018). The paper should position its bound relative to IBP-style guarantees.\n\n4. **Robust token selection/attention**: Robust attention mechanisms (Zhai et al., 2020), adversarially robust ViTs (Bai et al., 2021; Mo et al., 2022). These address perturbation sensitivity in attention-based selection.\n\n5. **Token importance scoring in LLMs**: H2O (Zhang et al., 2023), StreamingLLM (Xiao et al., 2023), ScissorHands (Liu et al., 2023). KV-cache eviction methods face similar importance-ranking stability issues.\n\n6. **Structured pruning with robustness**: Ye et al. (2019, \"Adversarial Robustness vs. Model Compression\"), Gui et al. (2019). These study the interaction between compression and robustness in other settings.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. How does your Proposition 1 differ from a simple sufficient condition Delta_k > k*eps*L => no flip? Why is it stated probabilistically when the scorer is deterministic?\n\n2. How does your bound compare to CAGE's certified guarantee? Is it tighter, weaker, or covering a different aspect?\n\n3. DynamicViT's prediction module learns per-token importance scores via a small network applied to token features---how does ACIS differ architecturally and conceptually beyond the margin training objective?\n\n4. Have you measured whether ToMe-style token merging (which preserves information from pruned tokens) is inherently more robust than pruning, potentially reducing the need for margin maximization?\n\n5. The \"denoising effect\" (G3 < 1.0) is surprising. Could this simply be that 50% of SmolVLA's visual tokens are always redundant/harmful, and any good selector would achieve this? What happens with a simple attention-threshold pruner without margin training?\n\n6. What is the computational cost of estimating L at deployment time, and how sensitive is the bound to L estimation error?\n\n7. Multi-seed results are mentioned in the roadmap as needed but appear absent from the paper. Can you provide variance estimates across seeds?\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: 5/10\n- Quality: 6/10\n- Clarity: 7/10\n- Significance: 5/10\n- Relevance: 8/10\n- Overall: 5/10\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n### Summary Assessment\nMarginally below acceptance. The core idea (maximize importance margin for robust token compression) is sensible and the experimental results are promising, but the novelty is overstated relative to existing margin-based robustness methods and ViT token pruning literature. The theoretical contribution (Proposition 1) is a straightforward Lipschitz bound dressed up as \"certified robustness.\" The related work section is severely incomplete, failing to engage with the extensive ViT pruning literature and margin-based defense methods that are conceptual predecessors. The paper would benefit from: (1) honest positioning of the bound as a design criterion rather than a certification, (2) comprehensive engagement with ViT token pruning and large-margin robustness literatures, (3) clearer differentiation from CAGE. A revision addressing these could reach acceptance at a top venue."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a57dc77f911ee0c71 (use SendMessage with to: 'a57dc77f911ee0c71' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 47113\ntool_uses: 13\nduration_ms: 101396</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"808481c6-6a8a-4eae-a76b-0f8df33193a6","uuid":"f92e18bc-6368-4582-8fdd-5639dbc1272f","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:39:22.297Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Produce an independent, blind review.\n\n## CoRL Review Scale (1-10, OpenReview)\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal paper\n- 9: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough, rejection\n- 3: Clear rejection\n- 2: Strong rejection\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nReview dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations.\nCoRL acceptance rate: ~30%. The venue emphasizes robot learning with real or sim experiments, principled methods, and practical impact.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Importance Margin Maximization: A Robustness-Aware Framework for Vision-Language-Action Token Compression\n\n**Problem:** Token compression is essential for deploying VLAs on resource-constrained robots, but existing methods are brittle to input perturbations.\n\n**Method — IMM:**\n1. ACIS (Adaptive Compression Importance Scorer): lightweight cross-attention module (2.3M params) producing per-token importance scores via s_i = σ(qK_i^T/√d).\n2. Importance margin Δ_k = s_(k) − s_(k+1).\n3. Formal bound: P(top-k flip) ≤ k·ε·L/Δ_k (Proposition 1).\n4. Training: L_margin = −log(Δ_k) + λ·L_action.\n5. Two-stage training: Stage 1 (freeze backbone, train ACIS), Stage 2 (freeze ACIS, train LoRA).\n\n**Related Work (Section 2, very brief ~0.5 page):**\n- VLA Token Compression: FastV [1] (attention-weighted), VLA-IAP [2] (input-adaptive pruning), VLA-Cache [3] (KV-cache eviction). \"Focus exclusively on efficiency under clean conditions and provide no robustness analysis.\"\n- Adversarial Robustness for VLAs: RobustVLA [5] (Jacobian regularization), ProGAL-VLA (perturbation-aware fine-tuning). \"Neither line of work connects robustness to the compression mechanism itself.\"\n- Certified Robustness: Randomized smoothing [6]. \"Our bound certifies the selection mechanism, requiring no inference-time modification.\"\n- Also cites: [4] \"Less is More Until It Breaks\" (adversarial vulnerabilities of VLA compression), [7] CAGE (compression-aware gradient estimation), [8] SmolVLA, [9] OpenVLA, [10] LIBERO.\n\n**Key experimental results:**\n- LIBERO-Plus 7-dim perturbation: IMM recovers 78% of robustness gap (37.3%→50.3% vs 53.1%)\n- Near-lossless 50% compression on SmolVLA (G3=0.87), controlled degradation on OpenVLA (G3=1.09)\n- Compression-agnostic plugin: +53pp preservation on FastV/VLA-IAP/VLA-Cache\n- Pareto-dominates AT, RS, Jacobian reg on clean+robust+compression+cost\n- Two-stage training eliminates co-adaptation (G3 3.29→0.87)\n- Formal bound validated, never violated, ~6.2x tightness ratio\n- 1.62x inference speedup, negligible ACIS overhead\n\n**Paper is 6 pages (compact format) with 10 references.**\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty of the core idea.** Is \"maximizing the importance margin for robust compression\" genuinely new? How does it relate to margin-based methods in other domains (classification margins, SVM, certified robustness)?\n2. **Positioning relative to related work.** Is the related work section comprehensive enough? Are important baselines or related methods missing?\n3. **Formal contribution.** Is Proposition 1 a meaningful theoretical advance, or is it a straightforward application of Lipschitz continuity to top-k selection?\n4. **Differentiation from closest works.** How does IMM compare to [4] (Less is More Until It Breaks), [5] (RobustVLA), and [7] (CAGE)? Is the novelty gap clear?\n5. **Broader context.** Token pruning/merging in vision transformers (e.g., ToMe, DynamicViT) has a large literature. Is this sufficiently cited?\n6. **Claims vs evidence.** Are the paper's novelty claims (first robustness-aware framework, compression-agnostic plugin, certified bound) adequately supported?\n7. **Writing quality and clarity.** Is the paper well-written for a 6-page format?\n\nStructure your output as:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, be specific]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, major vs minor]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Specific papers/areas that should be cited or discussed]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: X/10\n- Quality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Summary Assessment\n[1-2 sentences: accept/reject leaning and why]\n```\n","agentId":"a57dc77f911ee0c71","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have sufficient material to write a comprehensive review. Let me produce the review based on my analysis of both paper versions (the \"best\" optimistic scenario and the \"median\" realistic scenario), the project memory, and the paper framing document.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper proposes Importance Margin Maximization (IMM), a training framework that maximizes the gap between retained and pruned token importance scores in VLA token compression, yielding a formal top-k stability bound. A two-stage training protocol (freeze backbone/train ACIS, then freeze ACIS/train LoRA) eliminates co-adaptation and enables near-lossless or beneficial 50% compression. The idea of connecting margin-based robustness to